it is an Easter like no other for our  troops overseas  military chaplains coming up with unique  ways to mark this holy day safely  including drive-up communion right after  the live-streamed mass at a u.s. post in  japan joining us now the man behind that  idea a lieutenant colonel Donna Oki  chaplain for the US Army Garrison at  Camp Zama in Japan chaplain good morning  thanks so much for doing this we saw  what you were doing or who's not our  viewers would love yes happy Easter to  you we thought our viewers would love to  hear what you are doing for our troops  overseas on this Easter Sunday yes we're  doing some remarkable things some  creative things doing things a little  differently than we normally do but the  communities really responded well to it  you mentioned the drive-up Communion our  Catholic priest father Irizarry he came  up with that idea and because it's so  important to receive Communion and at  first I thought the I laughed and then I  thought well that's just crazy enough to  work  and really just how inspired and indeed  the communities responded well the cars  lined up every Sunday after Mass they  stay in their vehicles and they are all  able to receive Communion they receive  the blessing from father Irizarry and  also our Protestant folks are also doing  the same thing as well it's just a  matter of thinking creatively thinking  differently we don't have a game plan we  can fall upon from last year it's just  people coming up with good ideas and  implementing those ideas and taking  taking advantage of what there is still  available for us to do even as we are  practicing social distancing chaplain  what is the troop morale throughout all  of this  our morale is really high our folks are  100% behind leadership and people  understand exactly what they need to do  to keep themselves safe and more  importantly also to keep one another  safe this is a community effort it's  very collaborative and  people have kept their spirits high  sometimes it's really difficult to  especially see the news from back in the  states I don't think there is any family  here that is not impacted by what's  happening in America but yet we keep our  hopes high we're very resilient and we  stay strong it's a very positive message  and something wonderful to hear on this  Easter morning chaplain and we've been  showing pictures there of camp zama and  and the services that have been going on  these drive up services and even little  Easter bags were there to hand out so  you pull up in your car just like the  drive-in movie and you tune into the  station there and you hear this service  and you go away with your Easterner bag  leave us off with the response that  you've been getting to all this it's  been fantastic people really responded  well they loved it they loved the idea  for those of us who remember drive-ins  yes it has that feel of people drove up  they cut their Easter bag that  volunteers put together and we made sure  was was done with great care to make  sure we didn't spread the virus if it's  not done very carefully and they tuned  in to FM 88.9 and were able to hear us  through the transmitter and stayed  together in with one another inside  their guitars but still together as a  community as well it's so important to  keep even as we're practicing social  distancing that we still have that sense  of community is still there and I think  people really have the responses they  really felt like they had an opportunity  to worship together as a group even as  we were separate in our cars and giving  it over the radio there is a sense of  belonging and togetherness that we so  long for and it's so important for us as  a part of our faith to practice chaplain  aqui you're also the tenant Colonel  Donald aqui we thank you for your  service thank you for everything that  you're doing for our troops and for the  community there to keep their their  spirits high during these difficult  times Thank You chaplain thank your God  bless
 

